#**********************************************************************#
#  Shock formation: a velocity hump steepens into a shock.             #
#                                                                      #
#  Three layers of aluminum, all at rest but the middle one, which     #
#  holds a Gaussian hump of velocity: 1.0 km/s at its center, 0 at     #
#  its faces (normal 0.5 0.125, a spread of 0.5 mm in a 4 mm layer).   #
#  The layers are one material, so their contacts reflect nothing:     #
#  they only mark where the hump is. At t = 0 the pressure is 0, and   #
#  the hump splits in two (the Riemann invariants u + P/Z and          #
#  u - P/Z): a compression pulse running right (u about 0.5 km/s, P    #
#  about 8 GPa) and a tension pulse running left (P about -7 GPa).     #
#                                                                      #
#  The compression pulse steepens on its way through C. Its top runs   #
#  faster than its foot (wave speed c + u grows with u, about c0 +     #
#  2 S1 u for weak waves), so its front tilts forward until it is a    #
#  shock, while its back tilts the other way and spreads: the pulse    #
#  becomes a triangle with a shock at its front, and decays as it      #
#  runs. The front's steepest slope, about 0.30 km/s per 0.5 mm,       #
#  closes in t = 1/(2 S1 slope), about 0.6 us: a shock by x = 3 mm.    #
#                                                                      #
#  Watch it in the pressure movie (25 frames, <title>.mv_p_*.bmp) and  #
#  in the scope views of C's tracers at 1, 3, 6, 10 and 15 mm: the     #
#  rise sharpens from one to the next. Units: mm, us, g/cc, km/s,      #
#  GPa. Spall is off (see spall_strength below).                       #
#**********************************************************************#
Begin bitwhiz
 deck_version = 1

 Begin Run
  title = steepening_hump
  t_end = 3.5                   # us: the pulse's front nears C's free
                                # surface (20 mm at about 5.6 km/s)
 End Run

 Begin Output
  plot_dt      = 0.01           # us between snapshots
  movie_frames = 25             # the pressure movie, 0 to 3.5 us
  tracer_dt    = 0.002          # us between tracer rows (a shock's rise
                                # is a few ns at this mesh)
  colormap     = viridis

  tracer = c01  C@0.05          # x =  1 mm: the pulse as it leaves B
  tracer = c03  C@0.15          #       3 mm: about where it turns a shock
  tracer = c06  C@0.3           #       6 mm
  tracer = c10  C@0.5           #      10 mm
  tracer = c15  C@0.75          #      15 mm
 End Output

 Begin Solver
  method = AV                   # BL, BQ, CFL: the equation sheet's
 End Solver

 Begin Material Al              # aluminum (the ME 697 class deck's values)
  R0 = 2.78
  C0 = 5.30
  S1 = 1.34
  G0 = 2.00
  spall_strength = 100          # GPa: spall off. A wave demo, not a
                                # fracture study: the tension pulse in A
                                # is about 7 GPa; real aluminum breaks at
                                # about 1 to 2 (dynamic)
 End Material

 Begin Stack
  resolution = 50               # zones/mm: 300 + 200 + 1000 = 1500
                                # cells, 25 across the hump's spread
  origin     = B:C              # x = 0 where C begins (the hump's front
                                # foot)

  Begin Layer
   name     = A                 # at rest: the tension pulse runs into
   material = Al                # it, and its free surface throws it
   width    = 6.0               # back as compression, well behind
  End Layer

  Begin Layer
   name     = B                 # the hump
   material = Al
   width    = 4.0
   xvel           = 0.3133      # km/s: B's mean velocity. With the
                                # amplitude left at xvel, u = xvel*f, and
                                # f's peak is 1/(0.125*sqrt(2*pi)) =
                                # 3.1915, so the hump's top is 1.000 km/s
                                # and its feet (4 spreads out) 0.0001
   xvel_profile   = normal 0.5 0.125
  End Layer

  Begin Layer
   name     = C                 # at rest: where the shock forms
   material = Al
   width    = 20.0
  End Layer
 End Stack
End bitwhiz

Notes (ignored by the parser):
- Try a wider hump (normal 0.5 0.25) or a lower one (xvel 0.15): the
  shock forms later and farther, t about 1/(2 S1 slope).
- Turn spall on (delete spall_strength) to see the tension pulse break A.
- method = RS steepens it the same way, with a sharper front.
